1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,350
Chapter 57: Gastrointestinal Disorders.

3
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:32,937
General Considerations Upper Gastrointestinal Tract Disorders Hyperemesis Gravidarum Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease Peptic Ulcer Disease Small Bowel And Colon Disorders Acute Diarrhea Inflammatory Bowel Disease Intestinal Obstruction Appendicitis References During normal pregnancy, the gastrointestinal (GI) tract and its appendages undergo remarkable anatomical, physiological, and functional alterations.

4
00:00:32,937 --> 00:00:43,612
These changes, which are discussed in detail in Chapter 4 (p. 70), can appreciably alter clinical findings normally relied on for diagnosis and treatment.

5
00:00:43,612 --> 00:00:49,487
Moreover, as pregnancy progresses, GI findings become more difficult to assess.

6
00:00:49,487 --> 00:00:59,250
The clinical examination is often obscured by a large uterus that displaces abdominal organs and can alter the location and intensity of pain and tenderness.

7
00:00:59,250 --> 00:01:01,612
General Considerations.

8
00:01:01,612 --> 00:01:04,487
# Diagnostic Techniques.

9
00:01:04,487 --> 00:01:15,037
Endoscopy Several endoscopic methods can be used to evaluate the gastrointestinal (GI) tract during pregnancy without reliance on radiographic techniques.

10
00:01:15,037 --> 00:01:26,337
With fiberoptic endoscopic instruments, the esophagus, stomach, duodenum, and colon can be inspected (Savas, 2014; Song, 2018).

11
00:01:26,337 --> 00:01:40,612
The proximal jejunum also can be studied, and the ampulla of Vater cannulated to perform endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography—ERCP (Fogel, 2014; Hedström, 2017).

12
00:01:40,612 --> 00:01:47,337
Experience in pregnancy with video capsule endoscopy for small-bowel evaluation remains limited .

13
00:01:47,337 --> 00:01:57,612
Normal pregnancy-related slowing of GI motility and thus increased transit time as well as the recorder capsule’s electromagnetic field are theoretical concerns.

14
00:01:57,612 --> 00:02:03,862
Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy is used for diagnosis and management of several problems.

15
00:02:03,862 --> 00:02:09,125
Common bile duct exploration and drainage are used for choledocholithiasis .

16
00:02:09,125 --> 00:02:17,687
Sclerotherapy and placement of percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) tubes also are performed endoscopically.

17
00:02:17,687 --> 00:02:23,137
Colonoscopy is indispensible for viewing the entire colon and distal ileum.

18
00:02:23,137 --> 00:02:36,837
Except for the midtrimester, reports of colonoscopy during pregnancy are limited, but most results suggest that it should be performed if necessary (De Lima, 2015; Ludvigsson, 2017).

19
00:02:36,837 --> 00:02:43,112
Pregnancy indications include chronic abdominal pain, hematochezia, and diarrhea .

20
00:02:43,112 --> 00:02:51,725
Bowel preparation is completed using polyethylene glycol electrolyte (GoLYTELY) or sodium sulfate (Suprep) solutions .

21
00:02:51,725 --> 00:02:58,600
With these, most women avoid serious dehydration that may cause diminished uteroplacental perfusion.

22
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:06,200
In select cases, tap water enemas may be an alternative for rectosigmoidoscopy to avoid some of these risks.

23
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:19,200
Endoscopic procedures should be performed when indicated and ideally in the second trimester (American Society for Gastrointestinal Endoscopy, 2012; Ludvigsson, 2017).

24
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:26,700
A multidisciplinary approach with obstetricians, gastroenterologists, and anesthesiologists is prudent.

25
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:32,425
Table 57-1 outlines preprocedural considerations in pregnancy.

26
00:03:32,425 --> 00:03:34,987
Table 57-1.

27
00:03:34,987 --> 00:03:47,125
Preprocedural Considerations for Gastrointestinal Endoscopy During Pregnancy Noninvasive Imaging Techniques The ideal technique for GI tract imaging during pregnancy is abdominal sonography.

28
00:03:47,125 --> 00:03:53,300
However, the gravid uterus may obscure abdominal organs making evaluation difficult.

29
00:03:53,300 --> 00:04:03,862
Magnetic resonance (MR) imaging allows inspection of the abdomen and retroperitoneal space yet avoids radiation exposure from computed tomography (CT) .

30
00:04:03,862 --> 00:04:14,350
Examples are MR cholangiopancreatography (MRCP) and MR enterography (MRE) (Oto, 2009; Stern, 2014).

31
00:04:14,350 --> 00:04:21,200
These and other imaging modalities, and their safe use in pregnancy, are discussed in Chapter 49.

32
00:04:21,200 --> 00:04:30,200
Laparotomy and Laparoscopy Surgery is lifesaving for certain GI conditions—perforative appendicitis being the most common example.

33
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:36,675
Intraabdominal surgery is the most frequently performed nonobstetrical procedure during pregnancy .

34
00:04:36,675 --> 00:04:43,975
Laparoscopic procedures have replaced traditional surgical techniques for many abdominal disorders during pregnancy .

35
00:04:43,975 --> 00:04:55,287
These are discussed in detail with descriptions of surgical technique in Chapter 49 (p. 867) and in Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd edition .

36
00:04:55,287 --> 00:04:57,262
Guidelines for

37
00:04:57,412 --> 00:05:10,337
laparoscopy during pregnancy have been provided by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019) and the Society of American Gastrointestinal and Endoscopic Surgeons .

38
00:05:10,287 --> 00:05:12,974
# Nutritional Support.

39
00:05:12,974 --> 00:05:24,562
Specialized nutritional support can be delivered enterally, usually via nasogastric tube feedings, or parenterally by venous catheter access, either peripherally or centrally.

40
00:05:24,562 --> 00:05:34,912
When possible, enteral alimentation is preferable because it has fewer serious complications (Hoffer, 2018; Stokke, 2015).

41
00:05:34,912 --> 00:05:42,399
In obstetrical patients, very few conditions prohibit enteral nutrition as a first effort to prevent catabolism.

42
00:05:42,399 --> 00:05:53,849
For extreme cases, such as recalcitrant hyperemesis gravidarum, percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy with a jejunal port (PEG-J tube) has been described.

43
00:05:53,849 --> 00:05:59,449
Parenteral nutrition provides nutrients when the intestinal tract must be quiescent.

44
00:05:59,449 --> 00:06:07,374
Peripheral parenteral nutrition (PPN) administers dilute nutrient admixtures and is appropriate for short-term use.

45
00:06:07,374 --> 00:06:22,662
Central venous access is necessary for central parenteral nutrition (CPN) because its hyperosmolarity requires rapid dilution in a high-flow vascular system (Robinson, 2018; Worthington, 2017).

46
00:06:22,662 --> 00:06:30,999
These solutions provide 24 to 40 kcal/kg/d, principally as a hypertonic glucose solution.

47
00:06:30,999 --> 00:06:35,187
Various conditions may prompt CPN during pregnancy .

48
00:06:35,187 --> 00:06:43,349
The most common indications are short bowel syndrome and dysmotility disorders, and feeding duration averages 33 days .

49
00:06:43,349 --> 00:06:45,949
Table 57-2.

50
00:06:45,949 --> 00:06:54,599
Some Conditions Treated with Enteral or Parenteral Nutrition During Pregnancya Parenteral nutrition complications are frequent and may be severe .

51
00:06:54,599 --> 00:07:04,612
The most common is catheter sepsis, and Folk (2004) reported a 25-percent incidence in 27 women with hyperemesis gravidarum.

52
00:07:04,612 --> 00:07:13,524
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention provides detailed guidelines to prevent catheter-related sepsis (O’Grady, 2011).

53
00:07:13,524 --> 00:07:21,749
Other maternal complications include upper-extremity venous thrombosis, volume overload, and reversible liver dysfunction.

54
00:07:21,749 --> 00:07:30,624
Perinatal complications are rare, however, fetal subdural hematoma caused by maternal vitamin K deficiency has been described .

55
00:07:30,624 --> 00:07:38,574
Appreciable morbidity is also associated with long-term use of a peripherally inserted central catheter (PICC).

56
00:07:38,574 --> 00:07:47,299
Again, infection is the most common serious long-term complication (Hoffer, 2018; Holmgren, 2008).

57
00:07:47,299 --> 00:08:00,524
In a series of 84 such catheters inserted in 66 pregnant women, Cape and coworkers (2014) reported a 56-percent complication rate, and bacteremia was the most frequent.

58
00:08:00,524 --> 00:08:08,687
For short-term nutrition lasting a few weeks, PICC placement and its greater benefit-versus-risk ratio seems reasonable .

59
00:08:08,687 --> 00:08:12,024
Upper Gastrointestinal Tract Disorders.

60
00:08:12,024 --> 00:08:15,112
# Hyperemesis Gravidarum.

61
00:08:15,112 --> 00:08:22,774
Mild to moderate nausea and vomiting are especially common in pregnant women until approximately 16 weeks’ gestation .

62
00:08:22,774 --> 00:08:31,787
In a small but significant proportion of these, however, it is severe and unresponsive to simple dietary modification and antiemetics.

63
00:08:31,787 --> 00:08:45,149
Hyperemesis gravidarum is defined variably as severe unrelenting nausea and vomiting that produces weight loss, dehydration, ketosis, alkalosis from loss of hydrochloric acid, and hypokalemia.

64
00:08:45,149 --> 00:08:48,624
Acidosis develops from partial starvation.

65
00:08:48,624 --> 00:08:57,887
In some women, transient hepatic dysfunction develops, and biliary sludge accumulates (Table 58-1, p. 1031).

66
00:08:57,887 --> 00:09:04,374
Other causes should first be considered because hyperemesis gravidarum is a diagnosis of exclusion.

67
00:09:04,374 --> 00:09:10,024
Study criteria are not standardized, and thus population incidences vary .

68
00:09:10,024 --> 00:09:14,749
However, an ethnic or familial predilection has been described .

69
00:09:14,749 --> 00:09:28,174
In several population-based studies, the hospitalization rate for hyperemesis gravidarum was 0.5 to 2.1 percent (Fiaschi, 2019; Vikanes, 2013).

70
00:09:28,174 --> 00:09:35,424
Up to 25 percent of those hospitalized in a previous pregnancy for hyperemesis will again require admission .

71
00:09:35,424 --> 00:09:41,962
The etiopathogenesis of hyperemesis gravidarum is unknown and is likely multifactorial.

72
00:09:41,962 --> 00:10:00,749
High or rapidly rising serum levels of pregnancyrelated hormones such as human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), estrogen, progesterone, placental growth hormone, prolactin, thyroxine, and adrenocortical hormones are implicated (London, 2017; Verberg,

73
00:10:00,916 --> 00:10:14,016
2005). Other associated hormones include ghrelins, leptin, nesfatin-1, and peptide YY 3–36 (Albayrak, 2013; Gungor, 2013).

74
00:10:13,966 --> 00:10:20,228
Superimposed on this hormonal cornucopia are many biological and environmental factors.

75
00:10:20,228 --> 00:10:31,853
Moreover, in some but not all severe cases, interrelated psychological components play a major role (Mitchell-Jones, 2017; Senturk, 2017).

76
00:10:31,853 --> 00:10:49,966
Factors that increase the risk for admission include hyperthyroidism, previous pregnancy complicated by hyperemesis, diabetes, GI illnesses, some restrictive diets, and asthma (Fell, 2006; Fiaschi, 2016; Mullin, 2012).

77
00:10:49,966 --> 00:11:01,003
An association of Helicobacter pylori infection has been proposed, but evidence is inconclusive (Goldberg, 2007; London, 2017).

78
00:11:01,003 --> 00:11:10,228
Chronic marijuana use may cause the similar cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome (Alaniz, 2015; Andrews, 2015).

79
00:11:10,228 --> 00:11:23,741
And for unknown reasons— perhaps estrogen-related—a female fetus raises the risk but smoking and obesity decrease it by 1.5-fold (Cedergren, 2008; Fiaschi, 2016).

80
00:11:23,741 --> 00:11:35,166
Some have reported a relationship between severe hyperemesis gravidarum and anemia, venous thromboembolism (VTE), gestational hypertension, and preeclampsia .

81
00:11:35,166 --> 00:11:40,166
An association between hyperemesis and preterm birth is unclear .

82
00:11:40,166 --> 00:11:49,591
Koren and colleagues (2018) suggested that prolonged vomiting may cause metabolic abnormalities that interfere with fetal brain development.

83
00:11:49,591 --> 00:11:53,903
No long-term maternal consequences follow hyperemesis.

84
00:11:53,903 --> 00:12:05,003
Specifically, two population-based studies found no unfavorable cardiovascular risks in Norwegian women (Fossum, 2017, 2018).

85
00:12:05,003 --> 00:12:14,778
Complications Vomiting may be prolonged, frequent, and severe, and a list of potentially fatal complications is given in Table 57-3.

86
00:12:14,778 --> 00:12:22,516
Various degrees of acute kidney injury from dehydration are encountered, and rhabdomyolysis may be contributory .

87
00:12:22,516 --> 00:12:32,366
Rarely, we and others have encountered women with renal failure requiring dialysis (Dayangan Sayan, 2018; Hill, 2002).

88
00:12:32,366 --> 00:12:36,178
Mallory-Weiss tears result from continuous retching.

89
00:12:36,178 --> 00:12:51,878
Other complications include pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum, diaphragmatic rupture, and gastroesophageal rupture—Boerhaave syndrome (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2018; Chen, 2012).

90
00:12:51,878 --> 00:12:54,503
Table 57-3.

91
00:12:54,503 --> 00:13:05,191
Some Serious and Life-Threatening Complications Reported with Hyperemesis Gravidaruma At least two serious vitamin deficiencies have been reported with hyperemesis in pregnancy.

92
00:13:05,191 --> 00:13:14,191
One is Wernicke encephalopathy from thiamine deficiency (Di Gangi, 2012; Palacios-Marqués, 2012).

93
00:13:14,191 --> 00:13:23,366
A systematic review reported that ocular signs, confusion, and ataxia were common, and approximately 60 percent had this triad .

94
00:13:23,366 --> 00:13:37,116
In some women, an abnormal electroencephalogram (EEG) may be seen, and usually MR imaging shows findings (Oudman, 2019; Vaknin, 2006; Zara, 2012).

95
00:13:37,116 --> 00:13:41,803
One woman had recurrent encephalopathy in a subsequent pregnancy .

96
00:13:41,803 --> 00:13:52,878
Maternal deaths have been described, and long-term sequelae include blindness, convulsions, and coma (Oudman, 2019; Selitsky, 2006).

97
00:13:52,878 --> 00:13:56,366
Vitamin K is a second potential deficiency.

98
00:13:56,366 --> 00:14:09,141
Maternal coagulopathy, fetal intracranial hemorrhage, and vitamin K embryopathy have been reported (Kawamura, 2008; Lane, 2015; Nijsten, 2021).

99
00:14:09,141 --> 00:14:16,366
Management One management algorithm for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy is shown in Figure 57-1.

100
00:14:16,366 --> 00:14:28,266
Most women with mild to moderate symptoms respond to outpatient therapy with any of several first-line antiemetic agents (Boelig, 2018, McParlin, 2016).

101
00:14:28,266 --> 00:14:36,316
One mainstay is Diclegis—a combination of doxylamine (10 mg) plus pyridoxine (10 mg).

102
00:14:36,316 --> 00:14:46,078
The usual dose is two tablets orally at bedtime, and it appears safe and effective (Briggs, 2017; Koren, 2014).

103
00:14:46,078 --> 00:14:52,328
If relief is insufficient, one additional first-morning tablet is added to the bedtime dose.

104
00:14:52,328 --> 00:15:02,116
This can be further escalated to include one first-morning, one midafternoon, and two bedtime tablets (Duchesnay Inc., 2018).

105
00:15:02,116 --> 00:15:08,028
At our institution, for cost savings, we prescribe these two agents individually.

106
00:15:08,028 --> 00:15:18,603
The formula is one half of a 25-mg Unisom (doxylamine) tablet plus a 25-mg vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) tablet.

107
00:15:18,603 --> 00:15:24,191
The same graduated dosing is used but does not exceed three total daily doses.

108
00:15:24,191 --> 00:15:26,778
Figure 57-1.

109
00:15:26,778 --> 00:15:30,028
Algorithm for outpatient and inpatient

110
00:15:30,196 --> 00:15:35,383
management of pregnancy nausea and vomiting, and hyperemesis gravidarum.

111
00:15:35,333 --> 00:15:46,633
Ondansetron (Zofran) is slightly more effective than the combination of doxylamine and pyridoxine (Oliveira, 2014; Pasternak, 2013).

112
00:15:46,633 --> 00:15:51,433
In some studies, the former has been associated with birth defects .

113
00:15:51,433 --> 00:16:00,658
However, after a recent systematic review of Cochrane and Reprotox databases, authors concluded that ondansetron is not teratogenic .

114
00:16:00,658 --> 00:16:07,183
Other drawbacks include potential maternal effects from prolonged QT-interval and serotonin syndrome.

115
00:16:07,183 --> 00:16:13,208
For these reasons it is preferably reserved for severe cases after 8 weeks’ gestation .

116
00:16:13,208 --> 00:16:24,758
When simple measures fail, intravenous (IV) crystalloid solutions are given to correct dehydration, ketonemia, electrolyte deficits, and acid-base imbalances.

117
00:16:24,758 --> 00:16:29,371
Hypokalemia is common, and cardiac arrest has been reported .

118
00:16:29,371 --> 00:16:34,996
No benefits are gained by infusing 5-percent dextrose along with crystalloids .

119
00:16:34,996 --> 00:16:47,321
Thiamine, 100 mg, is usually diluted in 1 L of the selected crystalloid to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy for women who require IV hydration and have vomited for more than 3 weeks .

120
00:16:47,321 --> 00:16:51,721
It is infused at the maintenance rate desired for patient hydration.

121
00:16:51,721 --> 00:16:59,933
This thiamine dose is provided daily for the next 2 to 3 days and is followed by IV multivitamins if IV hydration continues .

122
00:16:59,933 --> 00:17:06,958
If vomiting persists after rehydration and failed outpatient management, hospitalization is recommended .

123
00:17:06,958 --> 00:17:16,433
IV hydration is continued and antiemetics such as promethazine, prochlorperazine, chlorpromazine, or metoclopramide are given parenterally .

124
00:17:16,433 --> 00:17:24,521
Several antiemetics are associated with the long-QT syndrome, discussed in Chapter 52 (p. 936).

125
00:17:24,521 --> 00:17:28,821
Outpatient treatment regimens at the hospital also are effective .

126
00:17:28,821 --> 00:17:31,471
Table 57-4.

127
00:17:31,471 --> 00:17:44,471
Medications for Gastrointestinal Disorders in Pregnancy Some, but not all, studies indicate that treatment with glucocorticosteroids is not effective (Sridharan, 2020; Yost, 2003).

128
00:17:44,471 --> 00:17:50,071
They are recommended only for refractory cases because of their putative teratogenicity .

129
00:17:50,071 --> 00:17:59,658
With persistent vomiting after hospitalization, appropriate steps should be taken to exclude possible underlying diseases as a cause of hyperemesis.

130
00:17:59,658 --> 00:18:05,571
That said, in one study, endoscopy did not change management in 49 women .

131
00:18:05,571 --> 00:18:15,083
Other potential sources include gastroenteritis, cholecystitis, pancreatitis, hepatitis, peptic ulcer, and pyelonephritis.

132
00:18:15,083 --> 00:18:21,308
In addition, severe preeclampsia and fatty liver are more likely after midpregnancy.

133
00:18:21,308 --> 00:18:34,496
Although clinical thyrotoxicosis has been implicated as a cause of hyperemesis, it is more likely that abnormally elevated serum thyroxine levels are a surrogate for higher-than-average serum hCG levels .

134
00:18:34,496 --> 00:18:39,121
This is discussed further in Chapter 5 (p. 97).

135
00:18:39,121 --> 00:18:46,208
In our experiences, serum free thyroxine levels normalize quickly with hydration and emesis treatment.

136
00:18:46,208 --> 00:18:53,096
With treatment, most women will have a positive response and may be sent home with oral antiemetic therapy.

137
00:18:53,096 --> 00:18:58,746
The readmission rate is 25 to 35 percent in most prospective studies.

138
00:18:58,746 --> 00:19:07,446
Risks for anxiety and depression are increased in these women (Mitchell-Jones, 2017; Senturk, 2017).

139
00:19:07,446 --> 00:19:13,333
At Parkland Hospital, more than one third of women with hyperemesis required readmission.

140
00:19:13,333 --> 00:19:18,796
Patient characteristics had limited utility in predicting the risk of readmission.

141
00:19:18,796 --> 00:19:28,471
For example, maternal demographics, substance use, psychiatric illness, and initial hospitalization length were not associated with readmission .

142
00:19:28,471 --> 00:19:36,058
If psychiatric and social factors contribute to the illness, the woman usually improves remarkably while hospitalized .

143
00:19:36,058 --> 00:19:49,171
However, symptoms relapse in a fourth of these women, and some go on to develop posttraumatic stress syndrome (Christodoulou-Smith, 2011; McCarthy, 2011; Nurmi, 2018).

144
00:19:49,171 --> 00:19:54,496
For some women, hyperemesis may be an indication for elective termination .

145
00:19:54,496 --> 00:20:01,083
Grooten and colleagues (2017) showed that early enteral tube feeding has no advantages.

146
00:20:01,083 --> 00:20:11,658
However, in the small percentage of women who have recalcitrant vomiting after intensive therapy, consideration is given for enteral nutrition (p. 1013).

147
00:20:11,658 --> 00:20:21,321
Stokke and associates (2015) described successful use of nasojejunal feeding for up to 41 days in 107 such women.

148
00:20:21,321 --> 00:20:22,908
Use of

149
00:20:23,068 --> 00:20:29,355
sonography to confirm correct tube placement has been described and avoids radiation exposure .

150
00:20:29,305 --> 00:20:36,618
PEG-J tubes also have been reported (Saha, 2009; Schrag, 2007).

151
00:20:36,618 --> 00:20:47,068
Only a very few women will require parenteral nutrition (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2018; Yost, 2003).

152
00:20:47,068 --> 00:20:57,630
In a study of 599 women, however, Peled and coworkers (2014) reported that 20 percent required central parenteral nutrition.

153
00:20:57,630 --> 00:21:01,280
# Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease.

154
00:21:01,280 --> 00:21:06,830
Symptomatic reflux is seen in up to 15 percent of nonpregnant individuals .

155
00:21:06,830 --> 00:21:13,980
The spectrum of sequelae includes esophagitis, stricture, Barrett esophagus, and adenocarcinoma.

156
00:21:13,980 --> 00:21:20,243
The main symptom of reflux is pyrosis (heartburn), which is especially common in pregnancy.

157
00:21:20,243 --> 00:21:30,118
In one study, its prevalence rose from 26 percent in the first trimester to 36 percent in the second and 51 percent in the third trimester .

158
00:21:30,118 --> 00:21:40,993
The retrosternal burning sensation stems from esophagitis caused by gastroesophageal reflux of stomach acid, which follows relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter.

159
00:21:40,993 --> 00:21:44,768
The diagnosis is typically made from symptoms alone.

160
00:21:44,768 --> 00:21:55,943
Symptoms usually respond to abstaining from tobacco and alcohol, eating small meals, elevating the head of the bed, limiting postprandial recumbency, and avoiding “trigger” foods.

161
00:21:55,943 --> 00:22:00,718
These may include fatty foods, tomato-based foods, and coffee.

162
00:22:00,718 --> 00:22:04,018
Oral antacids are first-line therapy.

163
00:22:04,018 --> 00:22:12,330
If severe symptoms persist, a proton-pump inhibitor is given and an H2-receptor antagonist and sucralfate can be added .

164
00:22:12,330 --> 00:22:16,318
Both classes are generally safe for use in pregnancy .

165
00:22:16,318 --> 00:22:23,855
From preliminary data, proton-pump inhibitors taken early in pregnancy may increase the risk for preeclampsia .

166
00:22:23,855 --> 00:22:29,118
Sucralfate is the aluminum salt of sulfated sucrose and inhibits pepsin.

167
00:22:29,118 --> 00:22:36,368
Approximately 10 percent of the aluminum salt is absorbed, and this agent is considered safe for pregnant women .

168
00:22:36,368 --> 00:22:43,918
A 1-g sucralfate tablet is taken orally 1 hour before each of the three meals and at bedtime for up to 8 weeks.

169
00:22:43,918 --> 00:22:49,393
Antacids are not used within a half hour before or after sucralfate doses.

170
00:22:49,393 --> 00:22:55,468
Despite attempts with these options, if relief is not attained, endoscopy should be considered.

171
00:22:55,468 --> 00:22:59,918
Misoprostol is contraindicated because it stimulates labor .

172
00:22:59,918 --> 00:23:04,755
In nonpregnant patients, surgical fundoplication can be performed .

173
00:23:04,755 --> 00:23:16,530
Although the procedure is not done during pregnancy, Biertho and colleagues (2006) described 25 women who had undergone laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication before pregnancy.

174
00:23:16,530 --> 00:23:20,755
Only 20 percent had reflux symptoms during pregnancy.

175
00:23:20,755 --> 00:23:23,243
# Hiatal Hernia.

176
00:23:23,243 --> 00:23:28,355
The older literature is informative regarding hiatal hernias in pregnancy.

177
00:23:28,355 --> 00:23:41,505
Upper gastrointestinal radiographs performed in 195 women in late pregnancy showed that 20 percent of 116 multiparas and 5 percent of 79 nulliparas had a hiatal hernia .

178
00:23:41,505 --> 00:23:47,318
Of 10 women studied postpartum, hernia persisted in three at 1 to 18 months.

179
00:23:47,318 --> 00:23:52,430
The relationship of hiatal hernia with reflux esophagitis is not clear.

180
00:23:52,430 --> 00:24:01,268
One study demonstrated no association and showed that the lower esophageal sphincter functioned effectively even when displaced intrathoracically .

181
00:24:01,268 --> 00:24:09,130
Nevertheless, during pregnancy, hiatal hernias may cause vomiting, epigastric pain, and bleeding from ulceration.

182
00:24:09,130 --> 00:24:17,093
Schwentner (2011) reported severe herniation requiring surgical repair in a woman with a 12week gestation.

183
00:24:17,093 --> 00:24:26,593
Curran and coworkers (1999) described a 30-week pregnancy complicated by gastric outlet obstruction from a paraesophageal hernia.

184
00:24:26,593 --> 00:24:29,480
# Diaphragmatic Hernia.

185
00:24:29,480 --> 00:24:36,018
These are caused by herniations of abdominal contents through either the foramen of Bochdalek or Morgagni.

186
00:24:36,018 --> 00:24:39,668
Fortunately, they rarely complicate pregnancy.

187
00:24:39,668 --> 00:24:49,243
In one review of 18 pregnant women who developed acute obstruction within a diaphragmatic hernia, the maternal mortality rate was 45 percent .

188
00:24:49,243 --> 00:24:55,455
Herniation has been reported in one woman who had antireflux surgery in early pregnancy .

189
00:24:55,455 --> 00:25:07,255
Several case reports also describe spontaneous diaphragmatic rupture from elevated intraabdominal pressure during delivery (Chen, 2012; Sharifah, 2003).

190
00:25:07,255 --> 00:25:15,880
Surgical treatment is considered in symptomatic gravidas and is individualized according to gestational age and clinical setting (Ménassa,

191
00:25:16,036 --> 00:25:19,973
2019; Whang, 2018).

192
00:25:19,923 --> 00:25:22,136
# Achalasia.

193
00:25:22,136 --> 00:25:29,148
In this rare motility disorder, the lower esophageal sphincter fails to relax properly with swallowing.

194
00:25:29,148 --> 00:25:34,998
The esophageal muscularis also displays nonperistaltic contraction activity .

195
00:25:34,998 --> 00:25:44,361
The defect is caused by inflammatory destruction of the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus within smooth muscle of the lower esophagus and its sphincter.

196
00:25:44,361 --> 00:25:51,073
Postganglionic cholinergic neurons are unaffected, and thus sphincter stimulation is unopposed.

197
00:25:51,073 --> 00:25:55,436
Symptoms are dysphagia, chest pain, and regurgitation.

198
00:25:55,436 --> 00:26:02,286
Barium swallow radiography demonstrates bird beak or ace of spades narrowing at the distal esophagus.

199
00:26:02,286 --> 00:26:08,611
Endoscopy is performed to exclude gastric carcinoma, and manometry is confirmatory.

200
00:26:08,611 --> 00:26:17,373
During pregnancy, normal physiological relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter in women with achalasia theoretically should not occur.

201
00:26:17,373 --> 00:26:23,186
In one series, half of these women had worsened symptoms, but reflux is uncommon .

202
00:26:23,186 --> 00:26:28,173
In other pregnancy studies, most women did not have worsening symptoms .

203
00:26:28,173 --> 00:26:37,398
At least one maternal death was reported at 24 weeks’ gestation and perforation of a 14-cm-diameter megaesophagus was implicated .

204
00:26:37,398 --> 00:26:42,811
Management of achalasia includes a soft diet and anticholinergic drugs.

205
00:26:42,811 --> 00:26:55,711
With persistent symptoms, other options include nitrates, calcium-channel antagonists, and botulinum toxin A injected locally (Hooft, 2015; Kahrilas, 2018).

206
00:26:55,711 --> 00:27:02,736
Balloon dilation of the sphincter may be necessary, and 85 percent of nonpregnant patients respond .

207
00:27:02,736 --> 00:27:08,511
One caveat is that esophageal perforation is a serious complication of dilation.

208
00:27:08,511 --> 00:27:14,248
If dilation or medical therapy fail to provide relief, myotomy is considered.

209
00:27:14,248 --> 00:27:24,911
In one report, a 29week pregnant woman with achalasia was treated for 10 weeks with peripheral parenteral nutrition, and surgical myotomy was done postpartum .

210
00:27:24,911 --> 00:27:27,673
# Peptic Ulcer Disease.

211
00:27:27,673 --> 00:27:32,673
The lifetime prevalence of acid peptic disorders in women is 10 percent .

212
00:27:32,673 --> 00:27:36,736
Erosive ulcer disease involves the stomach and duodenum.

213
00:27:36,736 --> 00:27:47,636
Gastroduodenal ulcers commonly are caused by chronic gastritis from Helicobacter pylori, or they develop from nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drug (NSAID) use.

214
00:27:47,636 --> 00:27:53,836
Neither is common in pregnancy (McKenna, 2003; Weyermann, 2003).

215
00:27:53,836 --> 00:28:05,973
Rosen and coworkers (2021) reported 2535 pregnant women with peptic ulcer disease and found that complications were less frequent than in nonpregnant controls.

216
00:28:05,973 --> 00:28:12,436
Acid secretion also is important, and this underlies the efficacy of antisecretory agents.

217
00:28:12,436 --> 00:28:25,073
Natural gastroprotection during pregnancy probably originates from physiological changes that include reduced gastric acid secretion, decreased motility, and considerably increased mucus secretion .

218
00:28:25,073 --> 00:28:31,998
Despite this, ulcer disease may be underdiagnosed because of frequent treatment for reflux esophagitis .

219
00:28:31,998 --> 00:28:42,911
In the past 55 years at Parkland Hospital, during which time we have cared for more than 600,000 pregnant women, we have encountered very few who had proven ulcer disease.

220
00:28:42,911 --> 00:28:45,148
Perforation is rare .

221
00:28:45,148 --> 00:28:59,173
Before appropriate therapy was commonplace, Clark (1953) studied 313 pregnancies in 118 women with ulcer disease and noted a clear remission during pregnancy in almost 90 percent.

222
00:28:59,173 --> 00:29:02,173
However, benefits were short lived.

223
00:29:02,173 --> 00:29:08,023
Symptoms recurred in more than half by 3 months postpartum and in almost all by 2 years.

224
00:29:08,023 --> 00:29:14,598
The mainstay of management is eradication of H pylori and prevention of NSAID-induced disease.

225
00:29:14,598 --> 00:29:25,011
Antacids are usually self-prescribed, but other first-line therapy is a proton-pump inhibitor or H2-receptor blocker (see Table 57-4.

226
00:29:25,011 --> 00:29:29,673
Del Valle, 2018; Laine, 2012).

227
00:29:29,673 --> 00:29:34,611
Sucralfate can be added and provides a protective coating at the ulcer base.

228
00:29:34,611 --> 00:29:39,248
With active ulcers, a search for H pylori is undertaken.

229
00:29:39,248 --> 00:29:47,136
Diagnostic aids include the urea breath test, serological testing, fecal testing, or endoscopic biopsy.

230
00:29:47,136 --> 00:29:55,198
If any of these yield positive results, combination antimicrobial plus proton-pump inhibitor therapy is indicated .

231
00:29:55,198 --> 00:30:01,798
Several effective oral treatment regimens do not include tetracycline and can be used during pregnancy.

232
00:30:01,798 --> 00:30:15,873
One 14-day regimen includes clarithromycin, 500 mg twice daily, which is paired either with amoxicillin, 1000 mg twice daily or with metronidazole, 500 mg three

233
00:30:16,036 --> 00:30:17,836
times daily.

234
00:30:17,786 --> 00:30:21,961
These antibiotics are given with a proton-pump inhibitor .

235
00:30:21,961 --> 00:30:25,273
# Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding.

236
00:30:25,273 --> 00:30:31,523
In some women, persistent vomiting is accompanied by worrisome upper gastrointestinal bleeding.

237
00:30:31,523 --> 00:30:35,011
Occasionally, a peptic ulcer is the source.

238
00:30:35,011 --> 00:30:44,686
However, most of these women have small linear mucosal tears near the gastroesophageal junction—Mallory-Weiss tears (p. 1014).

239
00:30:44,686 --> 00:30:52,573
Bleeding usually responds promptly to conservative measures that include IV proton-pump inhibitors and topical antacids .

240
00:30:52,573 --> 00:31:00,586
Transfusions may be needed, and if bleeding persists, endoscopy is usually indicated (O’Mahony, 2007).

241
00:31:00,586 --> 00:31:09,773
With sustained retching, the less common, but more serious, esophageal rupture—Boerhaave syndrome—may develop from high esophageal pressure.

242
00:31:09,773 --> 00:31:12,661
Small Bowel And Colon Disorders.

243
00:31:12,661 --> 00:31:16,761
The small bowel has diminished motility during pregnancy.

244
00:31:16,761 --> 00:31:32,523
Lawson and colleagues (1985) showed that the small bowel mean transit times of 99, 125, and 137 minutes in each successive trimester, respectively, were slower than 75 minutes when nonpregnant.

245
00:31:32,523 --> 00:31:40,511
Muscular relaxation of the colon is accompanied by increased absorption of water and sodium that predisposes to constipation.

246
00:31:40,511 --> 00:31:47,561
This complaint is reported by almost 25 percent of women at some time during pregnancy and the puerperium .

247
00:31:47,561 --> 00:31:59,748
Symptoms are usually only mildly bothersome, and initial treatment includes increasing fluid intake (>8 glasses/d) and fiber consumption (20 to 35 g/d) .

248
00:31:59,748 --> 00:32:10,023
If these lifestyle modifications fail, bulk-forming agents such as methylcellulose may be added, and in severe cases the stimulant laxative bisacodyl.

249
00:32:10,023 --> 00:32:15,511
We have encountered several pregnant women who developed megacolon from impacted stool.

250
00:32:15,511 --> 00:32:20,986
These women almost invariably had chronically abused stimulatory laxatives.

251
00:32:20,986 --> 00:32:23,536
# Acute Diarrhea.

252
00:32:23,536 --> 00:32:28,948
The estimated monthly prevalence of diarrhea among adults is 3 to 7 percent .

253
00:32:28,948 --> 00:32:36,498
Diarrhea can be classified as acute (<2 weeks), persistent (2 to 4 weeks), and chronic (>4 weeks).

254
00:32:36,498 --> 00:32:43,811
Most cases of acute diarrhea are caused by infectious agents, and a third result from foodborne pathogens.

255
00:32:43,811 --> 00:32:52,448
The large variety of viruses, bacteria, helminths, and protozoa that cause diarrhea in adults also afflict pregnant women.

256
00:32:52,448 --> 00:32:57,548
These are usually accompanied by vomiting, fever, and abdominal pain.

257
00:32:57,548 --> 00:33:02,711
Some of these are discussed in Chapter 64 (p. 1197).

258
00:33:02,711 --> 00:33:07,773
Evaluation of acute diarrhea depends on its severity and duration.

259
00:33:07,773 --> 00:33:30,436
Some indications for evaluation include profuse watery diarrhea with dehydration, grossly bloody stools, fever >38°C, duration >48 hr without improvement, recent antimicrobial use, and diarrhea in the immunocompromised patient (Camilleri, 2018; DuPont, 2014).

260
00:33:30,436 --> 00:33:39,273
Cases of moderately severe diarrhea with fecal leukocytes or gross blood may be treated with empirical antibiotics rather than evaluation.

261
00:33:39,273 --> 00:33:48,223
However, risks and benefits should be considered, and it is reasonable to withhold treatment until stool testing is completed to exclude infection.

262
00:33:48,223 --> 00:33:55,411
Some features of the more common acute diarrheal syndromes and their treatment are shown in Table 57-5.

263
00:33:55,411 --> 00:33:58,173
Table 57-5.

264
00:33:58,173 --> 00:34:14,686
Etiology, Clinical Features, and Treatment of Common Acute Diarrheal Syndromes The mainstay of treatment is IV hydration using normal saline or Ringer lactate and potassium supplementation in amounts to restore maternal blood volume and to ensure uteroplacental perfusion.

265
00:34:14,686 --> 00:34:19,311
Vital signs and urine output are monitored for signs of sepsis.

266
00:34:19,311 --> 00:34:29,398
For moderately severe nonfebrile illness without bloody diarrhea or recent travel, antimotility agents such as loperamide (Imodium) may be useful.

267
00:34:29,398 --> 00:34:38,361
An initial 4-mg (two capsules) dose can be followed with another 2-mg capsule after subsequently passed watery stools.

268
00:34:38,361 --> 00:34:49,723
Dosages should not exceed 8 mg/d, and loperamide is not used for more than 48 h. Bismuth subsalicylate (Pepto-Bismol) also may alleviate symptoms.

269
00:34:49,723 --> 00:35:05,236
The recommended dosage is 30 mL (525 mg) of its liquid form or two tablets (263 mg/tablet) chewed well each 30 to 60 min and not to exceed eight doses in 24 h.

270
00:35:05,236 --> 00:35:08,423
The drug will produce harmless black stools .

271
00:35:08,423 --> 00:35:12,486
Judicious use of antimicrobial agents is warranted.

272
00:35:12,486 --> 00:35:16,586
For moderately to severely ill women, some recommend

273
00:35:16,756 --> 00:35:23,118
empirical treatment with ciprofloxacin, 500 mg twice daily for 3 days.

274
00:35:23,068 --> 00:35:27,243
Specific pathogens are treated as needed when identified .

275
00:35:27,243 --> 00:35:37,793
Syndromes for which treatment is usually unnecessary include those caused by Escherichia coli, staphylococcal species, Bacillus cereus, and norovirus.

276
00:35:37,793 --> 00:36:03,218
Severe illness caused by Salmonella species is treated with ciprofloxacin or trimethoprimsulfamethoxazole; by Campylobacter species with azithromycin; by Clostridioides difficile with oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin; and by Giardia species and Entamoeba histolytica with metronidazole (DuPont, 2014; McDonald, 2018; Rocha-Castro, 2016).

277
00:36:03,218 --> 00:36:12,943
Clostridioides difficile Infection Formally known as Clostridium difficile, this anaerobic gram-positive bacillus is transmitted by the fecal-oral route.

278
00:36:12,943 --> 00:36:18,718
In a study from China, 3.7 percent of normal gravidas were colonized .

279
00:36:18,718 --> 00:36:23,293
It is the most frequent nosocomial infection in the United States.

280
00:36:23,293 --> 00:36:34,293
In 2011, 453,000 cases of C difficile and 29,000 associated deaths were reported by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention .

281
00:36:34,293 --> 00:36:38,656
Its incidence in pregnant women has doubled during the past decade .

282
00:36:38,656 --> 00:36:48,931
The most important risk factor is antibiotic use, and the highest risk is with aminopenicillins, clindamycin, cephalosporins, and fluoroquinolones.

283
00:36:48,931 --> 00:36:57,181
Other risk factors include inflammatory bowel disease, immunosuppression, advanced age, and gastrointestinal surgery.

284
00:36:57,181 --> 00:37:03,481
Most cases are hospital-acquired, however, 10 percent are community-acquired cases .

285
00:37:03,481 --> 00:37:08,743
With severe colitis, the infection-related mortality rate is 5 percent.

286
00:37:08,743 --> 00:37:23,856
Laboratory diagnosis from a stool sample uses nucleic-acid amplification testing (NAAT) for C difficile itself or uses an enzyme-immunoassay-based algorithm for a unique C difficile enzyme or its toxins A and B .

287
00:37:23,856 --> 00:37:28,231
Less often, infection may be diagnosed endoscopically .

288
00:37:28,231 --> 00:37:39,031
Only patients with new-onset diarrhea and ≥3 unformed stool of unexplained etiology should be tested, and posttreatment testing is not recommended.

289
00:37:39,031 --> 00:37:44,743
Prevention is by soap-and-water hand washing, and infected individuals are isolated.

290
00:37:44,743 --> 00:37:58,443
Treatment consists of stopping the offending antibiotics and giving 10 days of oral vancomycin, 125 mg four times daily, or fidaxomicin (Dificid), 200 mg twice daily .

291
00:37:58,443 --> 00:38:02,893
The risk of recurrence after an initial episode is 20 percent.

292
00:38:02,893 --> 00:38:08,868
Fecal microbial transplantation may become standard for recurrent clostridial colitis .

293
00:38:08,868 --> 00:38:11,468
Figure 57-2.

294
00:38:11,468 --> 00:38:13,618
Causes of colitis.

295
00:38:13,618 --> 00:38:18,131
A. Ulcerative colitis with diffuse ulcerations and exudates.

296
00:38:18,131 --> 00:38:21,268
B. Crohn colitis with deep ulcers.

297
00:38:21,268 --> 00:38:25,918
C. Pseudomembranous colitis with yellow, adherent membranes.

298
00:38:25,918 --> 00:38:28,443
D. Ischemic colitis.

299
00:38:28,443 --> 00:38:35,031
Reproduced with permission from Song LM, Topazian M: Gastrointestinal endoscopy.

300
00:38:35,031 --> 00:38:44,193
In Jameson JL, Fauci AS, Kasper DL, et al (eds): Harrison’s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th ed.

301
00:38:44,193 --> 00:38:48,493
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2018.

302
00:38:48,493 --> 00:38:51,631
# Inflammatory Bowel Disease.

303
00:38:51,631 --> 00:38:58,593
Two presumably noninfectious forms of intestinal inflammation are ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease.

304
00:38:58,593 --> 00:39:03,056
Differentiation between these is important because treatment differs.

305
00:39:03,056 --> 00:39:10,418
That said, they both share common features, and sometimes are indistinguishable if Crohn disease involves the colon.

306
00:39:10,418 --> 00:39:18,443
The features shown in Table 57-6 permit a reasonably confident diagnostic differentiation in most cases.

307
00:39:18,443 --> 00:39:26,656
The etiopathogenesis is enigmatic in both, but a genetic predisposition is suspected, especially for Crohn disease.

308
00:39:26,656 --> 00:39:36,193
Inflammation is thought to result from dysregulated mucosal immune function in response to commensal microbiota, with or without an autoimmune component .

309
00:39:36,193 --> 00:39:38,993
Table 57-6.

310
00:39:38,993 --> 00:39:50,631
Some Shared and Differentiating Characteristics of Inflammatory Bowel Disease Ulcerative Colitis This is a mucosal disorder with inflammation confined to the superficial luminal layers of the colon.

311
00:39:50,631 --> 00:39:55,968
It typically begins at the rectum and extends proximally for a variable distance.

312
00:39:55,968 --> 00:40:04,593
In approximately 40 percent of cases, disease is confined to the rectum or the rectosigmoid, but 20 percent have pancolitis.

313
00:40:04,593 --> 00:40:10,368
For unknown reasons, prior appendectomy protects against ulcerative colitis .

314
00:40:10,368 --> 00:40:18,393
Endoscopic findings include mucosal granularity and friability that is interspersed with mucosal ulcerations and a

315
00:40:18,556 --> 00:40:23,006
mucopurulent exudate (see Fig. 57-2).

316
00:40:22,956 --> 00:40:30,256
Major symptoms of ulcerative colitis include diarrhea, rectal bleeding, tenesmus, and abdominal cramps.

317
00:40:30,256 --> 00:40:34,743
The disease is characterized by exacerbations and remissions.

318
00:40:34,743 --> 00:40:42,143
Toxic megacolon and catastrophic hemorrhage are particularly dangerous complications that may necessitate colectomy.

319
00:40:42,143 --> 00:40:48,693
Extraintestinal manifestations include arthritis, uveitis, and erythema nodosum.

320
00:40:48,693 --> 00:40:53,568
The risk of associated colon cancer approaches 1 percent per year.

321
00:40:53,568 --> 00:41:04,818
With either ulcerative colitis or Crohn disease, risks for VTE are higher in even asymptomatic women (Friedman, 2018; Mahadevan, 2019).

322
00:41:04,818 --> 00:41:15,881
Crohn Disease Also known as regional enteritis, Crohn ileitis, or granulomatous colitis, Crohn disease has more protean manifestations than ulcerative colitis.

323
00:41:15,881 --> 00:41:22,243
It involves not only the mucosa but also the deeper bowel layers (see Fig. 572).

324
00:41:22,243 --> 00:41:29,468
Lesions can be seen throughout the entire GI tract, from the mouth to the anus, but it typically is segmental .

325
00:41:29,468 --> 00:41:42,318
Approximately 30 percent of patients have only small-bowel involvement, 25 percent have isolated colonic involvement, and 40 percent have both, usually with the terminal ileum and colon involved.

326
00:41:42,318 --> 00:41:47,956
Perianal fistulas and abscesses develop in a third of those with colonic involvement.

327
00:41:47,956 --> 00:41:51,718
Symptoms depend on which bowel segment(s) is involved.

328
00:41:51,718 --> 00:42:01,093
Thus, complaints may include lower-right-sided cramping abdominal pain, diarrhea, weight loss, low-grade fever, and obstructive symptoms.

329
00:42:01,093 --> 00:42:08,643
The disease is chronic with exacerbations and remissions, and importantly, it cannot be cured medically or surgically.

330
00:42:08,643 --> 00:42:17,456
Approximately a third of patients require surgery within the first year after diagnosis, and thereafter, the rate is 5 percent per year.

331
00:42:17,456 --> 00:42:25,931
Reactive arthritis is common, and the GI cancer risk, although not as great as with ulcerative colitis, is increased substantially.

332
00:42:25,931 --> 00:42:35,506
Inflammatory Bowel Disease and Fertility Subfertility in women is linked to active disease, but normal fertility is likely with quiescent colitis .

333
00:42:35,506 --> 00:42:43,043
Subfertility may also be partially attributed to sulfasalazine, which causes reversible sperm abnormalities .

334
00:42:43,043 --> 00:42:50,006
For women requiring surgical resection, a laparoscopic approach has a higher subsequent fertility rate .

335
00:42:50,006 --> 00:43:00,593
With colectomy, however, although fertility is improved, up to half of women will be persistently infertile (Bartels, 2012; Lee, 2019a).

336
00:43:00,593 --> 00:43:07,181
Sexual function and fertility are only modestly affected by ileal pouch–anal anastomosis .

337
00:43:07,181 --> 00:43:18,318
Inflammatory Bowel Disease and Pregnancy Because ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease are relatively common in young women, these disorders are encountered with some frequency in pregnancy.

338
00:43:18,318 --> 00:43:23,081
In many studies, the outcomes are grouped together for both entities.

339
00:43:23,081 --> 00:43:35,106
For example, in a study from Australia, Shand and colleagues (2016) reported a prevalence of 1 inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) case in 320 births.

340
00:43:35,106 --> 00:43:39,056
In this regard, some generalizations can be made.

341
00:43:39,056 --> 00:43:45,281
First, consensus supports that pregnancy does not increase the likelihood of an IBD flare .

342
00:43:45,281 --> 00:43:56,281
Indeed, in a 10-year surveillance of women in the European Collaborative on Inflammatory Bowel Disease, the likelihood of a flare during pregnancy was lower than the preconceptional rate .

343
00:43:56,281 --> 00:44:04,106
Although most women with quiescent disease in early pregnancy do not have relapses, when a flare develops, it may be severe.

344
00:44:04,106 --> 00:44:15,593
Also, and as discussed subsequently, active disease in early pregnancy increases the likelihood of disease relapse during pregnancy (de Lima-Karagiannis, 2016).

345
00:44:15,593 --> 00:44:20,881
In general, most usual treatment regimens may be continued during pregnancy.

346
00:44:20,881 --> 00:44:28,781
Diagnostic evaluations should be undertaken if needed to direct management, and surgery should be performed if indicated .

347
00:44:28,781 --> 00:44:43,631
Fecal calprotectin, an inflammatory biomarker in stool samples, is a valid tool in pregnancy to identify IBD flares (Julsgaard, 2017; Lichtenstein, 2018; Rubin, 2019).

348
00:44:43,631 --> 00:44:53,368
At first glance, it appears that adverse pregnancy outcomes are increased with IBD (Boyd, 2015; Getahun, 2014).

349
00:44:53,368 --> 00:44:59,943
Initially, this was attributed to the fact that most studies included women with either form of disease.

350
00:44:59,943 --> 00:45:04,518
Specifically, Crohn disease was noted to be linked to excessive

351
00:45:04,684 --> 00:45:06,421
morbidity.

352
00:45:06,371 --> 00:45:14,971
But, according to Reddy (2008) and others, these adverse outcomes were in women with severe disease and multiple recurrences.

353
00:45:14,971 --> 00:45:29,321
Indeed, in the prospective European case-control ECCO-EpiCom study of 332 pregnant women with IBD, outcomes were similar in women with ulcerative colitis or Crohn disease compared with unaffected pregnant women .

354
00:45:29,321 --> 00:45:40,096
Still, women with active disease during pregnancy have an increased frequency of preterm births (Kammerlander, 2017; Shand, 2016).

355
00:45:40,096 --> 00:45:45,171
Importantly, perinatal mortality rates are not appreciably increased.

356
00:45:45,171 --> 00:45:48,209
Ulcerative Colitis and Pregnancy.

357
00:45:48,209 --> 00:45:57,146
In one review of 755 pregnancies, colitis that was quiescent at conception worsened in approximately a third of pregnancies .

358
00:45:57,146 --> 00:46:08,059
In women with active disease at the time of conception, approximately 45 percent worsened, 25 percent remained unchanged, and only 25 percent improved.

359
00:46:08,059 --> 00:46:20,346
These observations are similar to those described in a review by Miller (1986) as well as in later reports (de Lima-Karagiannis, 2016; Oron, 2012).

360
00:46:20,346 --> 00:46:25,846
Osteoporosis is a significant complication in up to a third of affected women.

361
00:46:25,846 --> 00:46:33,621
Thus, vitamin D—800 IU daily—and calcium—1200 mg daily—are given.

362
00:46:33,621 --> 00:46:43,084
Folic acid, 2 to 4 mg orally daily, is recommended preconceptionally and during the first trimester for neural-tube defect prevention .

363
00:46:43,084 --> 00:46:48,034
This high dose counteracts the antifolate actions of sulfasalazine.

364
00:46:48,034 --> 00:46:53,309
Flares may be caused by psychogenic stress, and reassurance is important.

365
00:46:53,309 --> 00:47:00,446
Last, the venous thromboembolism risk is doubled, but thromboprophylaxis is not routinely provided .

366
00:47:00,446 --> 00:47:05,459
Management for colitis for the most part mirrors that outside of pregnancy.

367
00:47:05,459 --> 00:47:15,596
Treatment of active colitis and maintenance therapy incorporate drugs that deliver 5-aminosalicyclic acid (5-ASA), also known as mesalamine.

368
00:47:15,596 --> 00:47:24,646
Sulfasalazine (Azulfidine) is the prototype, and its 5-ASA moiety inhibits prostaglandin synthase in colonic mucosa.

369
00:47:24,646 --> 00:47:35,421
Others include olsalazine (Dipentum), balsalazide (Colazal), and delayed-release 5-ASA derivatives (Apriso, Asacol, Pentasa, Lialda).

370
00:47:35,421 --> 00:47:45,171
Glucocorticoids are beneficial and are given orally, parenterally, or by enema for moderate or severe disease that does not respond to 5-ASA.

371
00:47:45,171 --> 00:47:52,171
Corticosteroids provide a high remission rate for active disease, but they are not given for maintenance therapy .

372
00:47:52,171 --> 00:48:09,146
Recalcitrant disease is managed with immunomodulating drugs, including azathioprine, 6-mercaptopurine, cyclosporine, or tacrolimus, which all appear relatively safe in pregnancy (Briggs, 2017; Mozaffari, 2015).

373
00:48:09,146 --> 00:48:14,746
Importantly, methotrexate is teratogenic and contraindicated in pregnancy .

374
00:48:14,746 --> 00:48:20,884
In the past, biologics were reserved for recalcitrant, moderate to severe disease.

375
00:48:20,884 --> 00:48:31,021
Because of their considerable efficacy, however, these medications are now frequently given initially for this severity of disease to prevent future complications.

376
00:48:31,021 --> 00:48:36,871
These agents are antibodies against tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-α).

377
00:48:36,871 --> 00:48:42,284
Those recommended for IBD treatment are shown in Table 57-7.

378
00:48:42,284 --> 00:48:52,746
Women who begin pregnancy while taking biologics should continue these through pregnancy until several weeks before delivery to help avoid maternal infections from immunosuppression.

379
00:48:52,746 --> 00:48:56,921
These agents are administered IV or subcutaneously.

380
00:48:56,921 --> 00:49:10,046
Several studies support their safety in pregnancy, although there are concerns that their discontinuance may prompt a relapse (Chaparro, 2018; Friedman, 2018; Luu, 2018).

381
00:49:10,046 --> 00:49:24,434
Another worry is that they may cause immunosuppression in the newborn, and early neonatal vaccination with live-attenuated agents are delayed for at least 6 months (Esteve-Solé, 2017; Julsgaard, 2016).

382
00:49:24,434 --> 00:49:27,184
Table 57-7.

383
00:49:27,184 --> 00:49:34,946
Biologics Used to Treat Inflammatory Bowel Disease in Pregnancy Colorectal endoscopy is performed as indicated.

384
00:49:34,946 --> 00:49:38,609
Surgical management is tailored to disease severity .

385
00:49:38,609 --> 00:49:48,534
During pregnancy, colectomy and ostomy creation for fulminant colitis may be needed as a lifesaving measure, and it has been described during each trimester.

386
00:49:48,534 --> 00:49:57,846
Dozois (2006) reviewed 42 such cases and found that, in general, outcomes have been good with partial or complete colectomy.

387
00:49:57,846 --> 00:50:03,084
Parenteral nutrition is occasionally needed for women with prolonged exacerbations.

388
00:50:03,244 --> 00:50:13,769
For women with an ileal pouch and an anal anastomosis, frequency of bowel movements, fecal incontinence, and pouchitis may temporarily worsen with pregnancy.

389
00:50:13,719 --> 00:50:21,169
The last is an inflammatory condition of the ileoanal pouch probably due to bacterial proliferation and stasis.

390
00:50:21,169 --> 00:50:26,019
Pouchitis usually responds to ciprofloxacin or metronidazole.

391
00:50:26,019 --> 00:50:31,831
In one rare case, adhesions to the growing uterus led to ileal pouch perforation .

392
00:50:31,831 --> 00:50:36,944
In general, women with uncomplicated colitis can be delivered vaginally .

393
00:50:36,944 --> 00:50:41,556
That said, the cesarean delivery rate in these women is increased .

394
00:50:41,556 --> 00:50:50,181
It is controversial whether women who have had a prior proctocolectomy and ileal pouch–anal anastomosis can be safely delivered vaginally.

395
00:50:50,181 --> 00:51:01,119
Hahnloser (2004) reviewed delivery routes in women with 235 pregnancies before and 232 pregnancies after ileoanal pouch surgery.

396
00:51:01,119 --> 00:51:08,831
Functional outcomes were similar, and it was concluded that cesarean delivery should be reserved for obstetrical indications.

397
00:51:08,831 --> 00:51:20,931
To the contrary, following their systematic review, Foulon and coworkers (2017) recommended cesarean delivery with the caveat that uncomplicated vaginal delivery was safe.

398
00:51:20,931 --> 00:51:27,744
As discussed, quiescent ulcerative colitis likely has minimal adverse effects on pregnancy outcome.

399
00:51:27,744 --> 00:51:39,369
Modigliani (2000) reviewed perinatal outcomes in 2398 pregnancies and reported them to be not substantively different from those in the general obstetrical population.

400
00:51:39,369 --> 00:51:46,869
Specifically, the incidences of spontaneous abortion, preterm delivery, and stillbirth were remarkably low.

401
00:51:46,869 --> 00:51:58,356
These authors and others also describe a cesarean delivery rate that was substantially higher than that for normal controls (Burke, 2017; Mahadevan, 2015).

402
00:51:58,356 --> 00:52:08,819
The previously described ECCO-EpiCom study reported similar outcomes in 187 gravidas with ulcerative colitis compared with normal control women .

403
00:52:08,819 --> 00:52:11,369
Crohn Disease and Pregnancy.

404
00:52:11,369 --> 00:52:18,356
In general, Crohn disease activity during pregnancy is related to its status around the time of conception.

405
00:52:18,356 --> 00:52:29,044
In a cohort study of 279 pregnancies conceived by 186 women whose disease was inactive at conception, a fourth relapsed during pregnancy .

406
00:52:29,044 --> 00:52:36,006
In 93 with active disease at conception, however, two thirds either remained active or worsened.

407
00:52:36,006 --> 00:52:42,769
Other reviews describe similar findings (Miller, 1986; Oron, 2012).

408
00:52:42,769 --> 00:52:49,319
Calcium, vitamin D, and folic acid supplementation mirror that for ulcerative colitis.

409
00:52:49,319 --> 00:52:55,006
For maintenance during asymptomatic periods, no regimen is universally effective.

410
00:52:55,006 --> 00:53:01,769
Sulfasalazine is effective for some, but the newer 5ASA formulations are better tolerated.

411
00:53:01,769 --> 00:53:06,644
Glucocorticoids induce a 60- to 70percent remission rate .

412
00:53:06,644 --> 00:53:13,231
Prednisone therapy may control moderate to severe flares but is less effective for small-bowel involvement.

413
00:53:13,231 --> 00:53:23,206
Immunomodulators such as azathioprine, 6-mercaptopurine, cyclosporine, and tacrolimus are used for active disease and for maintenance.

414
00:53:23,206 --> 00:53:30,506
These appear relatively safe during pregnancy (Chaparro, 2018; Luu, 2018).

415
00:53:30,506 --> 00:53:37,506
As discussed in Chapter 8 (p. 152), methotrexate is contraindicated in pregnancy.

416
00:53:37,506 --> 00:53:50,144
As with ulcerative colitis, treatment with TNF-α inhibitors is often used initially for active Crohn disease and maintenance (Friedman, 2018; Mahadevan, 2019).

417
00:53:50,144 --> 00:53:55,231
Endoscopy or conservative surgery is indicated for complications .

418
00:53:55,231 --> 00:54:06,581
Patients with small-bowel involvement are more likely to require surgery for complications that include fistulas, strictures, obstruction, abscesses, and intractable disease.

419
00:54:06,581 --> 00:54:13,419
In an earlier study, an abdominal surgical procedure was required during 5 percent of pregnancies .

420
00:54:13,419 --> 00:54:18,444
Parenteral nutrition has been used successfully during severe recurrences.

421
00:54:18,444 --> 00:54:23,044
Those with an ileal loop colostomy may have significant problems.

422
00:54:23,044 --> 00:54:36,644
Unless there is a perianal fistula or active perianal disease, women with Crohn disease usually can undergo vaginal delivery without complications (Foulon, 2017; Mahadevan, 2019).

423
00:54:36,644 --> 00:54:44,231
As discussed, Crohn disease compared with ulcerative colitis is associated with higher adverse pregnancy outcome rates.

424
00:54:44,231 --> 00:54:48,069
Outcomes are probably related to disease activities.

425
00:54:48,069 --> 00:54:55,169
In a case-control Danish study, Norgård (2007) reported a twofold risk of preterm births.

426
00:54:55,169 --> 00:54:59,106
Dominitz (2002) reported a two- to

427
00:54:59,260 --> 00:55:09,572
threefold increased risk for preterm delivery, low birthweight, fetal-growth restriction, and cesarean delivery in 149 women with Crohn disease.

428
00:55:09,522 --> 00:55:17,547
Recall, however, that the ECCO-EpiCom study found pregnancy outcomes to be similar to those in unaffected women.

429
00:55:17,547 --> 00:55:20,360
# Ostomy and Pregnancy.

430
00:55:20,360 --> 00:55:26,385
A colostomy or an ileostomy can be problematic during pregnancy because of its location .

431
00:55:26,385 --> 00:55:36,760
In a report of 82 pregnancies in 66 women with an ostomy, stomal dysfunction was common, but it responded to conservative management in most cases .

432
00:55:36,760 --> 00:55:48,060
Surgical intervention was necessary, however, in three of six women who developed bowel obstruction and in another four with ileostomy prolapse—almost 10 percent overall.

433
00:55:48,060 --> 00:56:00,885
In this older study, only a third of 82 women underwent cesarean delivery, but Takahashi (2007) described six of seven cesarean deliveries in women with Crohn disease and a stoma.

434
00:56:00,885 --> 00:56:08,410
Although adhesions usually are involved with an obstructed ileostomy, the enlarging uterus may act to cause obstruction .

435
00:56:08,410 --> 00:56:15,697
Last, Farouk and coworkers (2000) reported that pregnancy did not worsen long-term ostomy function.

436
00:56:15,697 --> 00:56:18,535
# Intestinal Obstruction.

437
00:56:18,535 --> 00:56:25,935
The incidence of bowel obstruction is not increased during pregnancy, although it generally is more difficult to diagnose.

438
00:56:25,935 --> 00:56:34,772
Meyerson (1995) reported a 20-year incidence of 1 case in 17,000 deliveries at two Detroit hospitals.

439
00:56:34,772 --> 00:56:45,522
Of acute abdomen cases in pregnancy, adhesive disease leading to smallbowel obstruction was second only to appendicitis—15 versus 30 percent, respectively .

440
00:56:45,522 --> 00:56:56,360
Approximately half of cases are due to adhesions from previous pelvic surgery that includes cesarean delivery (Andolf, 2010; Lyell, 2011).

441
00:56:56,360 --> 00:57:04,860
Another 25 percent of bowel obstruction is caused by volvulus, which may involve sigmoid colon, cecum, or small bowel.

442
00:57:04,860 --> 00:57:13,360
These have been reported in late pregnancy or early puerperium (Al Maksoud, 2015; Bade, 2014).

443
00:57:13,360 --> 00:57:27,185
Bokslag (2014) and Wax (2013) and their colleagues described small-bowel obstruction in pregnancy following the currently popular Rouxen-Y gastric bypass, which is performed for weight loss.

444
00:57:27,185 --> 00:57:34,435
Intussusception is occasionally encountered (Bosman, 2014; Moliere, 2019).

445
00:57:34,435 --> 00:57:42,997
Bowel obstruction subsequent to colorectal surgery for cancer was increased threefold in women who had open versus laparoscopic surgery .

446
00:57:42,997 --> 00:57:50,710
Last, Serra and coworkers (2014) described a massive ventral hernia with intestinal obstruction.

447
00:57:50,710 --> 00:57:58,372
Most cases of intestinal obstruction during pregnancy result from pressure of the growing uterus on intestinal adhesions.

448
00:57:58,372 --> 00:58:10,347
This is more likely around midpregnancy when the uterus becomes an abdominal organ; in the third trimester when the fetal head descends; or immediately postpartum when uterine size acutely shrinks .

449
00:58:10,347 --> 00:58:21,472
Perdue (1992) reported that 98 percent of affected pregnant women had either continuous or colicky abdominal pain, and 80 percent had nausea and vomiting.

450
00:58:21,472 --> 00:58:28,797
Abdominal tenderness was found in 70 percent, and abnormal bowel sounds noted in only 55 percent.

451
00:58:28,797 --> 00:58:36,435
Plain abdominal radiographs following soluble contrast showed evidence of obstruction in 90 percent of affected women.

452
00:58:36,435 --> 00:58:50,785
Plain radiographs, however, are less accurate for diagnosing small-bowel obstruction, and we and others have found that CT and MR imaging can be diagnostic (Essilfie, 2007; Moliere, 2019).

453
00:58:50,785 --> 00:58:59,522
Colonoscopy can be both diagnostic and therapeutic for colonic volvulus (Dray, 2012; Khan, 2012).

454
00:58:59,522 --> 00:59:15,610
During pregnancy, mortality rates with obstruction can be excessive because of difficult and thus delayed diagnosis, reluctance to operate during pregnancy, and the need for emergency surgery (Firstenberg, 1998; Shui, 2011).

455
00:59:15,610 --> 00:59:26,672
In an older report of 66 pregnancies, Perdue and associates (1992) described 6-percent maternal and 26-percent fetal mortality rates.

456
00:59:26,672 --> 00:59:35,372
Two of the four women who died were in late pregnancy, and they had bowel perforation from sigmoid or cecal volvulus caused by adhesions.

457
00:59:35,372 --> 00:59:38,385
# Colonic Pseudo-Obstruction.

458
00:59:38,385 --> 00:59:44,822
Also known as Ogilvie syndrome, pseudo-obstruction is caused by adynamic colonic ileus.

459
00:59:44,822 --> 00:59:51,010
It is characterized by massive abdominal distention with cecal and right-hemicolonic dilation .

460
00:59:51,010 --> 00:59:54,485
Approximately 10 percent of all cases are

461
00:59:54,652 --> 01:00:00,989
associated with pregnancy, and its reported frequency is 1 case in 1500 births .

462
01:00:00,939 --> 01:00:08,839
The syndrome usually develops postpartum—90 percent after cesarean delivery—but it has been reported antepartum.

463
01:00:08,839 --> 01:00:15,577
In a review, perforation was common, especially if the cecal diameter exceeded 12 cm .

464
01:00:15,577 --> 01:00:25,889
More recently, treatment with an IV infusion of neostigmine, 2 mg, given during cardiac monitoring usually results in prompt decompression .

465
01:00:25,889 --> 01:00:33,627
In some cases, colonoscopic decompression is performed, and in others, laparotomy is needed for perforation .

466
01:00:33,627 --> 01:00:36,264
Figure 57-3.

467
01:00:36,264 --> 01:00:38,277
Ogilvie syndrome.

468
01:00:38,277 --> 01:00:44,102
Massive dilation of colon due to pseudo-obstruction in a woman following cesarean delivery.

469
01:00:44,102 --> 01:00:46,802
A. Abdominal radiograph.

470
01:00:46,802 --> 01:00:50,352
B. Axial image from computed tomography.

471
01:00:50,352 --> 01:00:52,802
# Appendicitis.

472
01:00:52,802 --> 01:00:57,802
The lifetime incidence for appendicitis ranges from 7 to 10 percent .

473
01:00:57,802 --> 01:01:03,827
Thus, evaluation for possible appendicitis is relatively common during pregnancy.

474
01:01:03,827 --> 01:01:16,502
Theilen and colleagues (2017) studied 171 such women during a 5-year period, but only 14 women ultimately were found to have pathologically confirmed appendicitis.

475
01:01:16,502 --> 01:01:35,377
After clinical and imaging evaluation, the frequency of suspected appendicitis drops and that of confirmed appendicitis in more than 8 million women ranged from 1 case in 1000 to 5500 births (Abbasi, 2014; Hée, 1999; Mazze, 1991).

476
01:01:35,377 --> 01:01:42,752
It is repeatedly—and appropriately—emphasized that pregnancy makes the diagnosis of appendicitis more difficult.

477
01:01:42,752 --> 01:01:58,477
Nausea and vomiting accompany normal pregnancy, but also, as the uterus enlarges, the appendix commonly moves upward and outward from the right lower quadrant (Baer, 1932; Erkek, 2015; Pates, 2009).

478
01:01:58,477 --> 01:02:05,664
Another often-stated reason for late diagnosis is that some degree of leukocytosis accompanies normal pregnancy.

479
01:02:05,664 --> 01:02:16,364
That said, Theilen and coworkers (2017) observed that a white cell count >18,000/μL made the diagnosis tenfold more likely.

480
01:02:16,364 --> 01:02:18,977
Neutrophilic shift is another .

481
01:02:18,977 --> 01:02:26,664
Pregnant women—especially in late gestation—frequently do not have clinical findings “typical” for appendicitis.

482
01:02:26,664 --> 01:02:37,077
Thus, it commonly is confused with cholecystitis, labor, pyelonephritis, renal colic, placental abruption, or uterine leiomyoma degeneration.

483
01:02:37,077 --> 01:02:43,564
Most reports indicate increasing morbidity and mortality rates with advancing gestational age.

484
01:02:43,564 --> 01:02:52,589
And, as the appendix is progressively deflected upward by the growing uterus, omental containment of infection becomes increasingly unlikely.

485
01:02:52,589 --> 01:02:58,214
It is indisputable that appendiceal perforation is more common during later pregnancy .

486
01:02:58,214 --> 01:03:10,402
In the studies by Andersson (2001) and Ueberrueck (2004), the incidence of perforation was approximately 8, 12, and 20 percent in successive trimesters.

487
01:03:10,402 --> 01:03:16,214
Diagnosis Persistent abdominal pain and tenderness are the most reproducible findings.

488
01:03:16,214 --> 01:03:22,864
Right lower quadrant pain is the most frequent, although pain migrates upward with appendiceal displacement .

489
01:03:22,864 --> 01:03:32,602
For initial evaluation, sonographic abdominal imaging is reasonable in suspected appendicitis, even if to exclude an obstetrical cause of pain .

490
01:03:32,602 --> 01:03:39,389
That said, graded compression sonography is difficult because of cecal displacement and uterine imposition .

491
01:03:39,389 --> 01:03:50,489
Appendiceal computed tomography is more sensitive and accurate than sonography to confirm suspected appendicitis (Katz, 2012; Raman, 2008).

492
01:03:50,489 --> 01:03:55,377
Specific views can be designed to diminish fetal radiation exposure .

493
01:03:55,377 --> 01:04:02,827
When available, MR imaging is the preferred modality for evaluation of suspected appendicitis in pregnancy .

494
01:04:02,827 --> 01:04:13,977
MR imaging has high diagnostic yield and accuracy, and it also provides alternative diagnoses (Fonseca, 2014; Theilen, 2015).

495
01:04:13,977 --> 01:04:24,439
One metaanalysis of 30 studies cited positive- and negative-predictive values of 96 and 99.5 percent, respectively, for MR imaging .

496
01:04:24,439 --> 01:04:30,977
Others report similar findings (Burke, 2015; Kave, 2019).

497
01:04:30,977 --> 01:04:38,777
However, Aguilera and colleagues (2018) found a sensitivity of only 18 percent in pregnant women.

498
01:04:38,777 --> 01:04:41,477
Figure 57-4.

499
01:04:41,477 --> 01:04:47,739
Magnetic resonance T2 flair image of acute appendicitis in a midtrimester pregnancy.

500
01:04:47,739 --> 01:04:54,252
The bright signal is inflammation and the block dot is a fecalith that can be seen in the midappendix (arrow).

501
01:04:54,252 --> 01:05:00,977
The fetal trunk (T) and leg (L) and amnionic fluid (A) are seen within the gravid

502
01:05:01,132 --> 01:05:04,044
uterus superior to the appendix.

503
01:05:03,994 --> 01:05:07,919
Reproduced with permission by Dr. Christina Herrera.

504
01:05:07,919 --> 01:05:14,069
Management When appendicitis is suspected, most recommend prompt surgical exploration.

505
01:05:14,069 --> 01:05:24,132
Although diagnostic errors may lead to removal of a normal appendix, surgical evaluation is preferable to postponed intervention and generalized peritonitis .

506
01:05:24,132 --> 01:05:30,969
In earlier reports, the diagnosis was verified in only 60 to 70 percent of pregnant women.

507
01:05:30,969 --> 01:05:40,994
As indicated above, however, with CT and MR imaging, these figures have improved (Duke, 2016; Theilen, 2015).

508
01:05:40,994 --> 01:05:47,619
Still and importantly, the accuracy of diagnosis is inversely proportional to gestational age.

509
01:05:47,619 --> 01:05:55,432
Currently, laparoscopic resection is almost always used to treat suspected appendicitis during the first two trimesters.

510
01:05:55,432 --> 01:06:08,194
In a report from a Swedish database of nearly 2000 laparoscopic appendectomies, perinatal outcomes were similar to those of more than 1500 open laparotomies done before 20 weeks’ gestation .

511
01:06:08,194 --> 01:06:17,032
Conversely, in their review, Wilasrusmee and coworkers (2012) reported a higher rate of fetal loss with laparoscopy.

512
01:06:17,032 --> 01:06:32,032
Authors of three more recent systematic reviews indicate that the level of evidence is not strong enough to demonstrate a preferred approach to appendectomy (Frountzas, 2019; Lee, 2019b; Walker, 2014).

513
01:06:32,032 --> 01:06:39,819
It has evolved that in many centers, laparoscopic appendectomy is also performed in most cases during the third trimester .

514
01:06:39,819 --> 01:06:53,119
This is encouraged by others, including the Society of American Gastrointestinal and Endoscopic Surgeons (Pearl, 2017; Sekar, 2019; Soper, 2011).

515
01:06:53,119 --> 01:07:02,532
Before exploration, IV antimicrobial therapy is begun, usually with a second-generation cephalosporin or third-generation penicillin.

516
01:07:02,532 --> 01:07:11,944
Unless there is gangrene, perforation, or a periappendiceal phlegmon, antimicrobial therapy can usually be discontinued after surgery.

517
01:07:11,944 --> 01:07:16,619
Without generalized peritonitis, the prognosis is excellent.

518
01:07:16,619 --> 01:07:21,369
Seldom is cesarean delivery indicated at the time of appendectomy.

519
01:07:21,369 --> 01:07:28,219
Uterine contractions are common, and although some clinicians recommend tocolytic agents, we do not.

520
01:07:28,219 --> 01:07:37,694
de Veciana (1994) reported that tocolytic use substantially increased the risk for pulmonarypermeability edema caused by sepsis .

521
01:07:37,694 --> 01:07:52,794
Antimicrobial versus Surgical Treatment Because of European studies, some advocate that many cases of appendicitis can be treated successfully with IV antimicrobials alone (Flum, 2015; Talan, 2019).

522
01:07:52,794 --> 01:08:06,757
In one study, 6 percent of pregnant women with appendicitis were treated medically, and these gravidas had “considerably” elevated risks for septic shock, peritonitis, and VTE compared with surgically managed cases .

523
01:08:06,757 --> 01:08:11,619
In another study of 20 women, failure rate was 25 percent .

524
01:08:11,619 --> 01:08:17,857
At this time, we discourage this practice until appropriate studies have been done with pregnant women.

525
01:08:17,857 --> 01:08:27,107
Certainly, if elected, such treatment should exclude women with obstructive appendicitis, and the threshold to convert to surgical treatment must be low.

526
01:08:27,107 --> 01:08:34,957
Pregnancy Outcomes Appendicitis increases the likelihood of preterm labor, especially if peritonitis has developed.

527
01:08:34,957 --> 01:08:48,569
Spontaneous labor after 23 weeks ensues with greater frequency following surgery for appendicitis compared with surgery for other indications (Ibiebele, 2019; Won, 2017).

528
01:08:48,569 --> 01:08:56,019
In one study, the fetal loss rate was 22 percent if surgery was performed after 23 weeks’ gestation.

529
01:08:56,019 --> 01:09:02,544
Three large population-based studies attest to the adverse outcomes from appendicitis in pregnancy.

530
01:09:02,544 --> 01:09:08,257
From the California State Inpatient Database, the fetal loss rate was 20 percent .

531
01:09:08,257 --> 01:09:21,407
A nationwide study from Taiwan found that risks for low birthweight and preterm delivery rose 1.5- to 2-fold when outcomes in 908 women with acute appendicitis were compared with those of controls .

532
01:09:21,407 --> 01:09:30,044
Last, an Australian population-based study reported an almost twofold increased incidence of preterm birth following appendectomy .

